From 0c871f353d41ed11d3fcf112ee94525b31c325c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 22:14:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Cover Guided dialogue scenes shot by shot
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A Guided scene is authored the way a scene is written: "these three are
in the corridor, and here is what they say." That is the right way to
write it and the wrong way to shoot it. Lip sync works on ONE visible
speaking face; a frame holding three faces gives the model no way to know
whose mouth to move. Which is why the eligibility rule reads

    owners.size === 1 && scene.roleIds.length === 1

and why every multi-person dialogue scene is skipped today and gets no
sync at all.

So the scene declares its cast and COVERAGE decides what is in each shot,
the way a production does it. Nothing here caps how many characters a
scene may hold: a scene with six people keeps all six, together in the
group shots, and when one of them speaks we cut to them. The cast drives
the coverage; the coverage never constrains the cast.

Coverage splits the SCRIPT rather than inventing a shot concept. Guided
is 1 script scene <-> 1 storyboard scene, and that identity is load-bearing
in positional scenes[index] pairing, immutable fingerprints, per-scene
funding and cast approval checkpoints — the machinery the repo's own notes
say must fail closed and must never strand paid work. A shot simply IS a
scene, which also makes it individually reviewable and individually
payable: the right unit for a user to approve.

Lines are never moved, merged, retimed or rewritten, so a scene at
0-6000ms with lines at 0-2500 and 2500-6000 becomes two scenes over the
same spans carrying the same lines. Narration is synthesised by flattening
every line onto one global timeline, so the WAV is what it would have
been, and narration reuse — which turns on transcript text, timing, line
ownership and voice bindings — is untouched. Coverage buys keyframes, it
never re-buys voices.

The craft that decides whether cut dialogue reads as a scene or a
slideshow is in here too. Eyelines are assigned by cast position and held
across every shot, so two speakers look at each other instead of both
staring the same way — the 180-degree rule, without which sync quality
cannot save you. Turns under 1.2s hold on the running shot instead of
flashing past. Adjacent lines by one speaker merge into one turn. A scene
that would gain nothing is left exactly alone, byte-identical, so its
preview and its paid work survive.

Expansion runs where the generated script becomes canonical, not at
enqueue: the approval check hashes the draft's script and the snapshot's
script and requires them equal, and backdrops are approved against scene
ids. Expanding later fails both, closed. Existing drafts keep their
scripts and are unaffected.

The 1-40 scene ceiling is spent greedily in script order, since a shot is
a scene and coverage multiplies them — a script too dense to cover keeps
the scenes its writer gave it rather than becoming one the validator would
reject on the next rebuild.

